00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
and now we were talking there about  england's coronavirus lockdown although  that will last at least until the start  of december wales so-called fire break  lockdown ends tomorrow  so to talk more about this we're joined  from cardiff by the first minister mark  drakeford thank you very much for being  on the program  so how's your fire break been a success  well we've said all along that we won't  know the effect of the fire break until  beyond the fire break period  but early indications particularly in  things like  travel tell us that people in wales  have done what we've asked to them  they've stayed at home they've worked  from home  they've avoided contact with other  people and when we come out of our fire  break period  tomorrow uh then we will need to go on  doing  everything we can to keep this virus  under proper suppression  it's good to see that you think the  compliance has been pretty good um but  at the same time if you look at the  numbers  weekly infection rates are rising in 14  of the 22 welsh councils  there are now more covered cases per 100  000 people  than when lockdown began it doesn't  sound like it's worked  but that was inevitable sophie wasn't it  the people who have been falling  ill during the two weeks of the fire  break period  were already infectious before the fire  break period  began uh we were absolutely certain that  we would see those numbers continue to  rise  during the fire break period itself it  is only in the couple of weeks  after the 9th of november that we will  see the impact of the fire brick  then is when we hope to see numbers  declining that is when we expect  the number of people going into our  hospitals to begin to go into reverse  i mean i understand with  hospitalizations and sadly of course  with deaths as well  there will be a lag and but i'm talking  about case numbers  is it really responsible to lift  restrictions when cases seem to be going  up  well the advice that we had when we went  into our 17-day fire break period was  but so long as we had a short but sharp  period of restrictions and really people  in wales in these 17 days  we've been back where we were at the end  of march and beginning of april in  terms of restrictions on people's lives  but provided we did everything we could  over those 17 days that would be  sufficient  and as i say it's not a surprise at all  that people who were falling ill  as the fire break began continue to feed  into the system  during the fire break period it's beyond  it we've always said  that we will see the pluses that we have  gained through the actions we have taken  and what if you don't see the outcome  that you're hoping  in the weeks ahead what if cases  continue to rise will you in reintroduce  a lockdown  well we think we have a path through to  christmas where we won't need to take  action  of this sort again it depends though  crucially  not simply on how people behaved during  the fire break period  but on how we all behave in the weeks  that follow it  i've been saying to people in wales that  the question each one of us must ask  ourselves  is not what can i do how far can i  stretch the rules  but what should i do what should i do to  keep myself and other people  safe and if we avoid contact with other  people if we travel only when we  need to if we work from home wherever we  can  we will build on what has been achieved  here over the last 17 days  and that will give us a path through to  christmas without needing  to go back into this extraordinary  period of restriction  now you've also said previously that you  don't want to return to this network of  local restrictions and that was in place  before the fire break but if you look at  some pockets in wales  there's clearly a concern about case  numbers method typical for example  now has the worst infection rate in the  whole of the united kingdom  and yet people living there are coming  out of lockdown should you not  be rethinking this  well what we've said is is that we will  not go back and rely  on a patchwork of local measures we are  going to have a new  national set of rules that are clearer  and i hope simpler  and therefore easier to follow that does  not mean  of course that if there are local  flare-ups that local action may not need  to be taken  uh but it will not be a reliance on  entirely local measures we ended up in  wales with quite a patchwork  of different local restrictions quite  hard for people to follow  a national set simpler clearer and  therefore we hope  easier for people to abide by is the key  way ahead  does that mean that local measures will  not be needed to supplement that in some  cases  no it doesn't and we've never ruled that  out interesting so it could be for  example that people  in murtha if those case numbers continue  to rise could see  local restrictions being imposed  well if there are local restrictions we  will target them at the cause of the  numbers  murtha as you know is the smallest  borough in the whole of wales  relatively low raw numbers drive quite  big  percentage and rate numbers if in murtha  there are specific  reasons we had for example earlier in  the pandemic a factory in mirtha  where there was an outbreak what we will  do instead of taking county-wide  measures  we will try to think of more targeted  things that we can do to bear  down on the cause of any local flare-ups  um interesting stuff um now of course  wales is coming out of lockdown  while england is just starting a  lockdown  and does that need to be restrictions  from people from wales traveling to  the rest of the united kingdom  well people from wales won't be allowed  to travel into england  because of the english lockdown rules  but we don't want to see people  traveling  people who need to travel for work and  medical appointments and so on of course  that's necessary travel but if you don't  have a good reason for traveling  our advice or strong advice to people in  wales is not to do it  the more people mingle and that includes  traveling  the more this virus likes it so our  strong advice to people in wales is  travel where only where you have a  really  necessary reason for doing so and that  certainly includes  at this point traveling across the  border  now you said a bit earlier in the  interview that you were hoping that the  action that you've already taken means  that it won't be necessary to  impose another wales lockdown before  christmas  and boris johnson has said that the four  nations are working on a joint approach  to the christmas period so what could  that look like  well can i just say i think it is really  important that we have a four nation  approach to christmas  so many families in wales have their  friends and other family members  in other parts of the united kingdom and  vice versa  so we were promised last week by michael  gove the minister in charge of the  cabinet office  but we will have another four nation  meeting in this coming week  so that we can pool ideas plan together  have a common approach to the christmas  period i really hope that meeting  materializes because it is only by  getting round the table and  sharing the position each one of us  faces  the challenges we have the common  solutions that we can craft  that we can get to i where i want us to  be and as i say that is a  single approach right across the united  kingdom for  everyone over the christmas period so  does that mean that you're hoping  and that people will be able to go and  see their loved ones their families over  christmas  well christmas is a very special time  for  all of us the restrictions people have  had to live with  are incredibly difficult and demanding  everybody  is tired and fatigued of coronavirus if  we could offer some respite  over the christmas period that is what  we would want to do but it needs to be a  common plan  and a common approach where the rules  are the same as much as we can make them  right across the united kingdom for that  very special period  but as i say the only way to do that is  for us to have that opportunity to meet  and talk together we've been promised  we'll have that opportunity this week  i really hope that the united kingdom  government is serious about this  and make certain that those  opportunities exist for us all  from your conversations with the  westminster government  do you think they are preparing to lift  restrictions over christmas  uh well we haven't had those discussions  yet and the history of coronavirus so  far has been  is that the united kingdom government is  a bit inclined to think about the united  kingdom  when there is a crisis that they  themselves are facing  then we are called often at short notice  around the table again as we were at the  start  of this uh previous week you can't do  that for christmas  you need a forward plan you need a plan  in advance  that's why i am very keen indeed to see  us meet next week  and if we do that then we will be able  to think together  about what is possible we will still be  in the grip of  a public health emergency that will not  have gone away  that is why thinking ahead planning  ahead and having a common plan  is so important okay mark drakeford  thank you very much for coming the  program  and explaining that welsh lockdown thank  you

